; Tutorial_7_Search_a_Library.inix -- TalkingBookTopics searched by full text: a phrase, both, leaving a word out, a word's
; start, a ranked saved search, an In Brief article shown as a web page, and books marked and written up as a wish list.
;
; WHY THIS WALK (27 November 2026, Jamal: tutorials should show good examples, not every feature). It replaces More
; Templates, a brief tour of five templates: the overview names them, and full-text search, the newest way to find
; things in DbDo, had no walk. Only the forms a reader reaches for are taught -- a phrase, &, !, a star, a saved search
; ranked best first. NEAR is heard inside the Guide Dogs search, not typed; ^, column groups and the index's own
; capital-letter syntax are left to the guide. Counts come from the November 2026 build; a live run confirms them.

[about]
Title=7 - Search a Library
Intro=TalkingBookTopics, sixty thousand books the National Library Service announced, searched by whole words: a phrase, two words at once, a word left out, a word's start; a saved search that puts the best matches first; one of the magazine's own news articles read as a web page; and books marked and written up as a wish list.
Setup=DbDo is open.
Homework=Find three books on a subject you love, mark them, and run the Wish List.

[step]

Say=One want: a few good books on a subject I care about, from a library of sixty thousand, and a list of them to keep.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Control plus Shift plus O, Open Template Database, lists the templates.
Key=Control+Shift+O
Hear=Open Template Database dialog
Hear=BookTrail
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=I choose TalkingBookTopics: every book the National Library Service announced in its magazine, Talking Book Topics, since 1997, with its author, narrator, book number and description. Enter.
Key=Enter
Hear=A Is for Alibi, Sue Grafton, Row 1 of 60159
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Keywords, Control plus K, finds letters anywhere, so art also finds start and party. In a library this size, whole words serve better. Control plus F, Filter Records.
Key=Control+F
Hear=Filter Records dialog
Hear=Keywords match button
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=A table with a full-text index offers Full-text match, the second button. F for Full-text.
Key=F
Hear=Full-Text Match dialog
Hear=Words to find: edit
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Two words typed together are a phrase. I type civil war and press Enter.
Key=Enter
Hear=623 records match.
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Only books that say civil war, not every book with civil in one place and war in another. Down Arrow.
Key=Down Arrow
Hear=Abraham Lincoln and the Civil War, Row 2 of 623
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=The same marks as Keywords work here: an ampersand means both, a bar means either, an exclamation point leaves a word out, and a star ends a word's start. Control plus F, and S for Start over.
Key=Control+F
Hear=Filter Records dialog
Hear=Start over button
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=S, then F for Full-text.
Key=S
Hear=Full-Text Match dialog
Hear=Words to find: edit, civil war
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=I type mystery, ampersand, scotland, ampersand, exclamation point, violence: mysteries set in Scotland, leaving out the violent ones. Enter.
Key=Enter
Hear=24 records match.
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=A planned misstep: a search for scot, hoping for Scotland. Full-text match finds whole words, so scot finds only scot. Scot with a star finds Scotland and Scottish.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Control plus Shift plus F clears the filter. A saved search can also rank its matches, best first, and read the passage that matched. Alt plus V, Invoke Script.
Key=Alt+V
Hear=Invoke Script dialog
Hear=Blindness
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Guide Dogs finds guide within three words of dog, in any order. I choose it and press Enter.
Key=Enter
Hear=Love on a Leash, Row 1 of 41
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=A word in a title counts most, then the author and the narrator, then the description, so the books most about guide dogs come first. Right Arrow to the passage.
Key=Right Arrow
Hear=passage, a blind woman and her guide dog travel
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Escape. The magazine also printed its own news, In Brief: new services, BARD, the move from cassettes to digital players. Control plus T, Open Table, and I choose updates.
Key=Control+T
Hear=Open Table dialog
Hear=authors
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=U for updates, and Enter. Each row is one article, newest first.
Key=Enter
Hear=Talking Book Topics now on BARD, Row 1 of 612
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=An article is written in Markdown, with headings, lists and links. Control plus Shift plus H, Preview Markdown in Web Browser, shows it as a web page.
Key=Control+Shift+H
Hear=Talking Book Topics now on BARD, web page
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=The browser's own keys read it, H for the next heading. Alt plus F4 closes the page and comes back. Now a list to keep: back on books, Semicolon marks a book I want.
Key=SemiColon
Hear=Marked
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Marks stay, so a list grows over days. Alt plus Shift plus R, Run Report, and I choose Wish List.
Key=Alt+Shift+R
Hear=Run Report dialog
Hear=Wish List
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Enter. Every marked book, with its author, narrator, book number, length and description, and a link to download it from BARD.
Key=Enter
Hear=Wish List, web page
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=What this walk taught. I name the command; the reader gives the key.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Keep the records whose words match.
Key=
Hear=Control plus F, then F

[step]

Say=Run a saved search, best first.
Key=
Hear=Alt plus V

[step]

Say=Show a Markdown field as a web page.
Key=
Hear=Control plus Shift plus H

[step]

Say=Mark a record.
Key=
Hear=Semicolon

[step]

Say=Write the marked records up.
Key=
Hear=Alt plus Shift plus R
